+// Every generated struct/enum conversion in this file (parseJ<Name> below)
+// resolves its Java class through this one helper, so this is also the one
+// place a fix needs to land to cover all of them, current and future.
+//
+// Plain JNIEnv::FindClass is unsafe here: these conversions run from
+// callback threads WebRTC/ntgcalls creates natively (e.g. the PeerConnection
+// signalling/network threads that carry onConnectionChange -- see
+// wrtc/src/interfaces/native_connection.cpp's network_thread()/
+// signaling_thread() PostTask calls), not threads Java ever started. Per
+// Android's own JNI FAQ (https://developer.android.com/training/articles/perf-jni#faq_FindClass,
+// also documented directly on webrtc::GetClass's declaration in
+// sdk/android/native_api/jni/class_loader.h): FindClass consults the
+// ClassLoader associated with the nearest Java frame on the calling thread's
+// stack. A thread merely AttachCurrentThread-ed from native code (which is
+// exactly what wrtc::utils::GetJNIEnv() -> webrtc::AttachCurrentThreadIfNeeded()
+// does -- the thread IS JVM-attached) has no such frame, so FindClass falls
+// back to the system/bootstrap ClassLoader, which cannot see any
+// app-packaged class -- io.github.pytgcalls.* included. That is a
+// ClassLoader-context defect, not a missing-attachment or missing-class one.
+//
+// webrtc::GetClass(env, name) is WebRTC's own fix for exactly this: it
+// resolves against a ClassLoader reference webrtc::InitClassLoader cached
+// from JNI_OnLoad's thread (a real Java thread, via System.loadLibrary),
+// safe to call from any later thread. ntgcalls' own JNI_OnLoad
+// (targets/android/app/src/main/jni/jni_onload.cpp) already calls
+// webrtc::InitAndroid(jvm), which calls InitClassLoader(...) internally
+// (see sdk/android/native_api/base/init.cc) -- so the correct ClassLoader is
+// already cached and ready; this helper simply wasn't using it.
 inline webrtc::ScopedJavaLocalRef<jclass> findClass(JNIEnv* env, const char* name) {
-    return webrtc::ScopedJavaLocalRef<jclass>::Adopt(env, env->FindClass(name));
+    return webrtc::GetClass(env, name);
 }
 
